title: The daemon's startup report
tags: Daemon
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: What the daemon prints on stderr while it loads its repositories — one repository, one phase, one event at a time.

The daemon reports its startup on stderr ''as it goes'': each repository is named
before its load starts, and its outcome — `[daemon] Loaded <name>`, or a
warning — is written before the next one is attempted. On a terminal the
announcement is a progress line. So a startup that takes long always says which
repository it is waiting for.

Within a repository, every ''phase'' announces itself before it runs, and gives its
duration when it ends (only past 10 ms, so that the instant ones do not bury the
one that costs):

```
[load music] open the key-value store…
[load music] probe case sensitivity…
[load music] load the schema…
[load music] load the metadata map…
[load music] replay the buffered filesystem events…
[load music] place the filesystem watches…
[load music] start the watcher…
```

The reading rule: ''the last line names the phase that is running''. Load work is
proportional to the repository, not the code; a phase can run for minutes on a
large one.

The replay reports ''inside'' itself, being the least predictable phase: how many
events it read, how many were left after compaction, then each event as it
applies it — and, for a directory that arrived whole (one event hiding a whole
subtree), the entries it has ingested so far:

```
[load photos]   1 event(s) buffered
[load photos]   1 event(s) after compaction
[load photos]   event 1/1: arrived /photos
[load photos]     scanning /photos: 2326 entries ingested
```

These lines are limited to one per second, the first and the last always shown.
One event is several steps of very different cost, and a step that ''stalls''
cannot report itself — the code that would is the code stuck in it — so a
''watchdog'' reports from outside, naming the current step once a second while it
stays current, and nothing while the steps flow:

```
[load home]   still on: event 2/426: modified /target/…/acl-manifests.json — refresh the stat fields (4.12s)
[load home]   event 2/426 took 5.14s
```

These timings stay on stderr: a client already watches a load as a task. What is
slow outside a load goes to the [[Slow log]]; what the daemon warns about goes to
the [[Health and diagnostics]] feed.
